{"id": "B4", "label": "Vascular / Structural Lesion (Epistaxis / Septal Perforation)", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.161, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Normal physical exam findings and absence of chronic symptoms strongly exclude vascular/structural lesions (B4)."]}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Bacterial Sinusitis with Septic Shock", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.30587758512366003, "posterior": 0.30048778646052093, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Hypotension and tachycardia provide weak evidence against uncomplicated bacterial sinusitis (B2.3), favoring systemic complications."], "evidence_against": ["Lack of distributive shock signs moderately reduces septic shock (B2.1) likelihood despite hemodynamic abnormalities."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "management_pathway", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Bacterial Sinusitis with Orbital Cellulitis", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.097, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Localized symptoms without facial deformities moderately reduce orbital cellulitis (B2.2) compared to systemic septic shock (B2.1)."]}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Bacterial Sinusitis without Complications", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.033, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Hypotension and tachycardia provide weak evidence against uncomplicated bacterial sinusitis (B2.3), favoring systemic complications."]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Unilateral Nasal Foreign Body with Secondary Infection", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.05823460491534771, "posterior": 0.05823460491534771, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Unilateral Nasal Foreign Body without Infection", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.04367595368651078, "posterior": 0.04367595368651078, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.2, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "management_pathway", "representative_diseases": []}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B2.1", "B2.1"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the pattern of worsening pain with purulent-hemorrhagic discharge support orbital cellulitis (B2.2) as a localized complication?", "summary": "Purulent-hemorrhagic discharge provides moderate support for localized complications like orbital cellulitis (B2.2), but localized nasal presentation reduces this likelihood."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the mild fever (37.5°C) without other systemic signs favor uncomplicated bacterial sinusitis (B2.3) over septic shock (B2.1)?", "summary": "Mild fever without organ dysfunction weakens septic shock (B2.1) and provides neutral evidence for uncomplicated sinusitis (B2.3)."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the hemorrhagic nasal discharge provide evidence for a vascular/structural lesion (B4) despite normal physical exam findings?", "summary": "Hemorrhagic discharge provides weak evidence for vascular/structural lesions (B4), but normal exam findings reduce this likelihood."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of agitation, confusion, or cool/pale extremities argue against septic shock (B2.1) despite hypotension and tachycardia?", "summary": "Lack of distributive shock signs moderately reduces septic shock (B2.1) likelihood despite hemodynamic abnormalities."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the localized nasal presentation and absence of facial deformities make orbital cellulitis (B2.2) less likely than septic shock (B2.1)?", "summary": "Localized symptoms without facial deformities moderately reduce orbital cellulitis (B2.2) compared to systemic septic shock (B2.1)."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of hypotension and tachycardia contradict uncomplicated bacterial sinusitis (B2.3) in favor of systemic complications?", "summary": "Hypotension and tachycardia provide weak evidence against uncomplicated bacterial sinusitis (B2.3), favoring systemic complications."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.622], ["B1", 0.28], ["B4", 0.096]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.581], ["B2.1", 0.306], ["B4", 0.226]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.431], ["B2.1", 0.3], ["B4", 0.161]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Hypotension (90/48 mmHg) and tachycardia (124/min) in a 7-year-old with systemic alarm signs (mild fever) and worsening sinusitis suggest septic shock. Bacterial superinfection (B2) has high danger score (0.8) and posterior probability (0.62), requiring immediate intervention.", "required_actions": ["Administer IV antibiotics for septic shock", "Initiate fluid resuscitation for hypotension", "Monitor for distributive shock progression"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "[static mode override] Active emergency interrupt (hypotension/tachycardia suggesting septic shock) requires immediate intervention. B2.1 (septic shock) has posterior 0.306 and danger 0.9, but definitive management (IV antibiotics/fluid resuscitation) cannot wait for further diagnostics. — emergency_override suppressed in static_diagnosis_qa mode"}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.45, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.30048778646052093, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Bleeding and infected vessel of Kiesselbach plexus"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Foreign body obstruction"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Nasopharyngeal carcinoma"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Septal perforation"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Sinusitis with bacterial superinfection"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Nasal Discharge', 'Sinus tachycardia', 'Nasal deformity'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B2", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.622}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2.1", "label": "Bacterial Sinusitis with Septic Shock", "posterior": 0.3, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 2, "disconfirming": 2, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Lack of distributive shock signs moderately reduces septic shock (B2.1) likelihood despite hemodynamic abnormalities."}, "action_intents": [{"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "confirm"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B4", "B2.2", "B1.1"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=80%, source=both]\n\nBacterial Sinusitis with Septic Shock vs Bacterial Sinusitis with Orbital Cellulitis:\n  Favours first:  agitation, anxiety, confusion or disorientation, cool and pale limbs, decreased mental status\n  Favours second: abnormality of the face, agenesis of lateral incisor, autosomal dominant inheritance, bilateral cleft lip, bilateral cleft palate\n\nBacterial Sinusitis with Septic Shock vs Unilateral Nasal Foreign Body with Secondary Infection:\n  Favours first:  agitation, anxiety, confusion or disorientation, cool and pale limbs, decreased mental status\n  Favours second: coughing, difficulty breathing, ear pain, facial pain, nasal bleeding\n\nBacterial Sinusitis with Septic Shock vs Unilateral Nasal Foreign Body without Infection:\n  Favours first:  agitation, anxiety, confusion or disorientation, cool and pale limbs, decreased mental status\n  Favours second: coughing, difficulty breathing, ear pain, facial pain, nasal bleeding\n\nBacterial Sinusitis with Orbital Cellulitis vs Unilateral Nasal Foreign Body with Secondary Infection:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the face, agenesis of lateral incisor, autosomal dominant inheritance, bilateral cleft lip, bilateral cleft palate\n  Favours second: coughing, difficulty breathing, ear pain, facial pain, nasal bleeding\n\nBacterial Sinusitis with Orbital Cellulitis vs Unilateral Nasal Foreign Body without Infection:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the face, agenesis of lateral incisor, autosomal dominant inheritance, bilateral cleft lip, bilateral cleft palate\n  Favours second: coughing, difficulty breathing, ear pain, facial pain, nasal bleeding\n\nRelated to Bacterial Sinusitis with Orbital Cellulitis: acute orbital inflammation, cellulitis (disease)\n\n[RAG context for 'A 7-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his parents for worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis' (score=0.57):]\n  Source: Daily outpatient intravenous antibiotic therapy for the management of paediatric periorbital cellulitis, a retrospective case series. > Epidemiology\n  Periorbital cellulitis can occur at any age but is particularly common in the pediatric population. This condition is more frequent than orbital cellulitis. Studies have shown that periorbital celluli\n[RAG context for 'A 7-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his parents for worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis' (score=0.58):]\n  Source: Periorbital and orbital cellulitis. > Epidemiology\n  Overall, orbital cellulitis is much more common in younger children than in older children or adults. This is because most cases of orbital cellulitis are preceded by bacterial rhinosinusitis or an up\n[RAG context for 'Initially' (score=0.55):]\n  Source: Periorbital and orbital cellulitis. > Pathophysiology\n  Most cases of orbital cellulitis are bacterial and often involve causative bacteria that can cause sinusitis, since direct extension through small perforations in the orbital septum is the most common\n[RAG context for 'Initially' (score=0.56):]\n  Source: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis. > Treatment / Management\n  Intravenous antibiotics are the mainstay of treatment for patients with orbital cellulitis or moderate-to-severe preseptal cellulitis. Recommended antibiotics include vancomycin and ampicillin-sulbact\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis after decongestant treatment → complicated bacterial sinusitis with septic shock progression → Bacterial Sinusitis with Septic Shock\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for signs of septic shock (systemic inflammation, hypotension, tachycardia, altered mental status) despite initial sinusitis diagnosis\n  nasal discharge with worsening pain in a patient with controlled asthma → secondary bacterial infection complicating viral sinusitis → Unilateral Nasal Foreign Body with Secondary Infection\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Investigate for unilateral nasal foreign body despite bilateral presentation, considering foul-smelling discharge as indirect evidence\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
